    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey professor, i'm trying to understand timbre better for my acoustics exam. how do overtones actually change the sound of a flute compared to just hearing the fundamental frequency?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Timbre refers to the unique \"tone color\" or \"sound quality\" of a sound, and overtones play a significant role in shaping a sound's timbre. When you hear a flute, its timbre is not just determined by its fundamental frequency (the lowest pitch), but also by the harmonic overtones that resonate along with it. These overtones, also known as harmonics, add complexity and texture to the sound, making it sound more rich and full-bodied. Think of overtones as \"sideband\" frequencies that reinforce and enrich the fundamental frequency, resulting in the distinctive timbre of the flute.",
      "source": "target",
